                        <p class=\"caption\">Classification algorithms act like detectives, sorting items like emails into categories such as \"inbox\" or \"spam\" based on learned patterns.</p>
                    </div>
                    
                    <p>That is the work of a type of algorithm called classification. It is like a detective, sorting each email into spam or not spam based on characteristics it has learned to identify.</p>
                    
                    <div class=\"info-box\">
                        <h3>How Classification Works</h3>
                        <p>Classification algorithms work by identifying patterns in data that distinguish different categories. They learn these patterns from labeled examples (supervised learning) and then apply what they\'ve learned to categorize new, unseen data.</p>
                        <p>Common classification algorithms include:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Decision Trees:</strong> Make decisions based on a series of questions</li>
                            <li><strong>Support Vector Machines:</strong> Find the optimal boundary between categories</li>
                            <li><strong>Random Forests:</strong> Combine multiple decision trees for better accuracy</li>
                            <li><strong>Neural Networks:</strong> Learn complex patterns through interconnected layers</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class=\"content-section\" id=\"regression\">
                    <h2>Regression Algorithms: Predicting Values</h2>
                    <p>Now, let\'s say you are house hunting. Ever wondered how real estate websites predict the price of a house?</p>
                    
                    <div class=\"image-container\">
                        <svg width=\"400\" height=\"220\" viewbox=\"0 0 400 220\">
