                    <p>The way we use a generative AI model depends entirely on our technical knowledge. Let\'s use an analogy of gardening to illustrate this.</p>
                    
                    <div class=\"key-concept\">
                        <h3>The Gardening Analogy for Model Usage</h3>
                        <p>There are different ways to engage with generative AI models, depending on your technical expertise:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>The Landscape Designer (Expert Level):</strong> Just as a landscape designer uses their extensive knowledge of gardening to plan and cultivate a garden from the ground up, tech experts can create custom AI models for complex tasks.</li>
                            <li><strong>The Home Gardener (Intermediate Level):</strong> Like a home gardener using seed packets and young plants, someone with basic tech knowledge might use pre-existing models from repositories like GitHub and adapt them for specific needs.</li>
                            <li><strong>The Indoor Plant Enthusiast (Beginner Level):</strong> Similar to using a simple indoor plant kit with instructions, users can access services like Microsoft Copilot without needing technical expertise.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    
                    <p>In all these scenarios, the level of control you want over the results from the generative AI model depends on your knowledge, resources, and specific needs. Each approach has its own benefits and can produce excellent results when used appropriately.</p>
                </section>

                <section class=\"content-section\" id=\"user-interaction\">
